$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}h(x)
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\dfrac{\sqrt{x+12}-3}{x+3}\qquad\gray{\text{This is the rule for }x\neq -3}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\dfrac{\sqrt{x+12}-3}{x+3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{x+12}+3}{\sqrt{x+12}+3}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\dfrac{x+12-3^2}{(x+3)(\sqrt{x+12}+3)}\qquad\gray{\text{Simplify}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\dfrac{\cancel{x+3}}{\cancel{(x+3)}(\sqrt{x+12}+3)}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel common factors}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\dfrac{1}{(\sqrt{x+12}+3)}
\\\\
&\small\text{(This is allowed because }x\neq -3)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{-3+12}+3}\qquad\gray{\text{Direct substitution}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{6}
\end{align}$

We obtained that if we set $h(-3)=\dfrac{1}{6}$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}h(x)=h(-3)$, which makes $h$ continuous at $x=-3$.

Since we already saw that $h$ is continuous for any other $x>-12$, we can determine that it's continuous for all $x>-12$.
